nius resides in my nostrils  and you know if we're going to say well  whose genius resides in their nostrils  if anyone it would be dogs wouldn't it  so  they  know well enough to eat up the corn  that's on the ground get something for  free and that's what they're doing  then we have a hegel reference here a  dog being recognized in and for itself  by a dog that's recognized in and for  itself in so far as they are two dogs  eating kettle corn  and you know what's the the reference  there well you know the the dialectic of  self-consciousness is about recognition  and  uh these two are recognizing each other  in some shared activity  um hegel doesn't really think  recognition is for animals but  that's okay this is a good one here we  have another hegel reference me and the  boys chowing down on the newest  contemporary hegelians book a bit of a  stretch you know i suppose you could say  that uh  hegelians work like that of hegel could  be considered just a heap of stuff in  which case you know if it's tasty enough  then you  mow down on it that doesn't really say  anything great about hegel of course  then we get uh two descartes references  and i suppose i should have seen these  coming first i snack therefore i am  and then uh i eat i exist and you notice  that one doesn't have a therefore  descartes original formulation of i  think  therefore i exist um  sometimes does sometimes doesn't have  the therefore right but there's a  connection between two different types  of things and this is kind of worth  dwelling on right snacking eating  uh is that part of our existence is that  how we know that we exist maybe not for  us human beings but perhaps that is so  absolutely central in the  life of an animal like a dog  the next one is a reference to a  scripture quote right this is something  that is at least at the very start  something said by  jesus and it's man shall not live on  bread alone but dogs do quite well on  corn apparently so  a little bit of twist the